+# The Daily Grind
+
+Run `npm install`, then `npm run build` and `npm test`. `npm start` builds and
+serves the application; deploy the `dist/` directory.
+
+The existing hero photograph is vendored in `assets/coffee-hero.jpg`. The build
+hashes those exact bytes with SHA-256 and embeds a content-addressed local URL
+and lowercase hash in the generated page. If the source asset is missing, the
+build downloads the original photograph once. Normal builds need no asset
+network access. To update the photograph, replace the source asset and rebuild.
+
+`src/load-asset.js` checks Cross-Origin Storage before fetching. Cache failures
+fall back to the network; policy denials disable further COS calls for the page.
+Successful downloads are written in full, and storage failures don't prevent
+displaying the photograph. This site's image deliberately keeps the default
+same-site sharing scope. Callers may choose a trusted origin array or, for a
+genuinely popular public resource, `'*'`. Even globally scoped resources can
+return a cache miss because of privacy or popularity gating. Multiple assets
+can be loaded with `Promise.all` over separate `loadAsset` calls.
+
+Browsers without COS use the same local network asset. Runtime code never hashes
+the asset or downloads it first to identify it. Strict TypeScript checking covers
+the browser JavaScript and the experimental COS API declarations. Tests simulate
+COS support and failures; they do not require a browser with experimental support.
